        "denialFound": "None found; the MP WCD department was not asked. The FPJ article carries no response from the government or the department. The CAG report text (which usually includes the government's reply) could not be found on cag.gov.in in this pass, and the web-search budget was used up. The absence is already recorded in c029.",
        "innocentReading": "\"Unregistered\" may mean the SHGs had not registered under the specific state or NRLM mechanism the scheme requires, not that they were fictitious. District officers may have chosen centralised kitchens to deliver meals in remote tribal districts (Alirajpur, Sheopur, Mandla) where there were not enough qualifying SHGs. Paying hired cooks and keeping no stock registers is a compliance failure, not proof that meals were never delivered. The ₹8.1 cr is the value of supplies from non-compliant SHGs, not an assessed loss or amount diverted.",
        "reason": "The source confirms every element of the label almost word for word: \"54 unregistered self-help groups (SHGs) were engaged in supplying HCM in seven of the 13 test-checked districts... These SHGs supplied meals worth Rs 8.1 crore to 252 anganwadi centres,\" and \"13 SHGs in Alirajpur, Sehore and Tikamgarh had neither kitchens nor offices at the addresses mentioned in bills.\" The period (\"ending March 2024\"), the actor (CAG) and the amount (Rs 8.1 crore, so no currency conversion) all match. The CAG report itself was not located, so this rests on one credible outlet reporting an audit document, which is the definition of `reported`. The tier is right. The claim frames the ₹8.1 cr as supply value, not loss, and implies no wrongdoing beyond what the audit says. Only minor fixes to the description are needed.",
