        "sourceCheck": "partially",
        "denialFound": "No denial found, and the department was asked but did not answer. Scroll: \"Despite several attempts, Scroll.in was unable to meet or speak with senior officials from the state's Women and Child Development department. Scroll.in's email queries to the officials in the Union Ministry for Women and Child Development also went unanswered.\" An anonymous ICDS official gave the state's rationale on the record: \"The Centre has fixed the rate of Rs 6 per child per day ... suppliers who feel they cannot produce the food at that rate need not apply for the tender.\" The tender also formally excludes private contractors, which is the state's implicit defence. https://scroll.in/article/848000/maharashtra-does-away-with-hot-cooked-meals-for-children-in-anganwadis",
        "innocentReading": "The 2015 MoWCD rule made states certify that meals met the 2009 fortification norms, and thousands of small SHGs cannot certify micronutrient levels. Factory-made fortified mixes that can be lab-tested were a way to comply. The per-child budget (₹6/day) stayed the same, and the tender formally barred private contractors. On this reading the switch was about compliance and quality control, not about moving money to particular firms. The loss of livelihood for SHGs was a side-effect, not the aim.",
        "reason": "The source supports the component facts: ₹575 cr annual business, the 15 Jul 2017 tender, 34 rural/tribal sectors, ~45,000 SHGs, 1.5 lakh women, Dipa Sinha's NFSA objection, and no answer from WCD. It does not support how the claim combines them. The ₹575 cr is the annual value going \"to the few who can manufacture the ready-to-cook meals\". The same source says the SHGs \"currently earn small amounts by serving hot cooked meals\". The label \"₹575 cr/yr business that 45,000 SHGs stood to lose\" is therefore a conjunction the source never states. The edge encoding (pmout, ministry:mh-wcd → ngo:mh-icds-mahila-mandals, a=575) is worse: it asserts a ₹575 cr flow to the SHGs, the opposite of the article. The d field also misquotes the child count: the source says 17 lakh children aged 0–6 in SNP, while the headline says 3 million children aged 3–6. The facts hold at \"reported\" (one credible outlet with the tender document in hand), but the edge as encoded is refuted until its amount and direction are fixed.",
