        "denialFound": "No denial is needed, because the claim reports the firm's own statement. The firm was asked: TC says \"The firm didn't respond to a request for comment Monday but confirmed the development after the publication of this story.\" It gave the market-maturity reason and said it had \"achieved our primary objective of catalysing impact\". The firm's own statement page (omidyargroup.com) returns 404 (recorded as a void).",
        "innocentReading": "A global impact investor closed a 13-year country programme after India's domestic VC and philanthropy grew. Its 2023 write-downs (Doubtnut, ZestMoney) also made the programme less attractive. The claim as worded implies no wrongdoing and makes no enforcement-causation assertion; that question sits separately in c013 as analytic.",
        "reason": "Every element of the claim appears word for word in the sources: start year 2010; \"about $673 million of cumulative assets under management\" as of July 2023 (TC, citing an investor presentation); exit announced 11/12 December 2023; the market-maturity reason (\"significant change in context… more Indian led philanthropic and venture capital than ever before\"); and the end-2024 transition (BT). The claim does not stack facts to imply something the sources don't say. Tier \"reported\" is right for a grant/investment edge whose amount comes second-hand from an investor presentation and a press summary. The announcement facts alone could reach documented, since TC, BT, Business Standard and YourStory each covered them. The claim survives, but its fields need fixing: the predicate and target mislabel mostly-equity capital as grants, the record wrongly says no grant amount was stated, and the AUM figure is not a flow to grantees.",
