        "sourceCheck": "partially",
        "denialFound": "A response exists, so c016's text is wrong. On 2025-08-21 MEA told Parliament, citing US Embassy data, that USAID gave no US$21m for voter turnout in India. My recollection is that this was a written Rajya Sabha answer by MoS Kirti Vardhan Singh, which said the Embassy's 2014-2024 USAID data showed no US$21m received and no voter-turnout activity implemented. I confirmed the event only from Google News RSS listings dated 2025-08-21/22. These headlines include the Indian Express (\"Citing US embassy, Govt confirms: No $21-million USAID poll grant in India\"), The Hindu (\"USAID didn't receive funding of $21 million for voter turnout in India: Embassy to MEA\"), NDTV (\"US Embassy Contradicts Trump's 'USAID Fund For Voter Turnout In India' Claim\"), The Wire (\"MEA Says US Embassy Denies Trump's Claim of $21m USAID Funds to Modi, All USAID Pacts with India Ended August 15\"), Scroll, TOI and BOOM. I could not open the article bodies (The Wire renders with JS; Google News links are encoded), so the MoS name and the 2014-2024 window are unconfirmed. Earlier, ThePrint (2025-03-17) reported that MEA had asked the US Embassy for details. The Washington Post (2025-02-22) called it \"a false DOGE claim\". I found no statement from CEPPS, IFES, IRI or NDI themselves; whether they were asked is unknown.",
        "innocentReading": "DOGE staff labelled a line from the CEPPS award portfolio as \"India\" when the US$21m voter-participation award was for Bangladesh. Trump repeated the label, and the figure then drifted to \"$18 million … to my friend Modi\". The boring explanation is a clerical mislabel amplified by political speech, not a hidden India grant. It fits every piece of evidence: the Bangladesh award records, \"no CEPPS projects in India since 2008\", and the US Embassy's own data given to MEA.",
        "reason": "Kill it as an edge; keep it as a narrative. The assertion is accurately sourced: both URLs say DOGE listed and cancelled \"$21 million for 'voter turnout in India'\", and BS quotes Trump. But the edge claims that USAID sent ₹178.5 crore to CEPPS for India. Its own killIf is met (\"USAID award records show the only US$21m CEPPS voter-participation grant was for Bangladesh\"). BS/Indian Express report it, and the asserting party's own government then withdrew it: the US Embassy told MEA, and MEA reported this to Parliament in August 2025. The claim's line \"contra c016 = no CEPPS/USAID response recorded\" is false, because the US government, which now administers what was USAID, did respond. Keeping the edge would also count c017's Bangladesh money twice and place it inside the India graph.",
