        "denialFound": "Not applicable as a denial, since the claim accuses no one. Party statements on record: DEA/FinMin annual report, via PTI (BS 2025-02-23): \"During the year, no funding was made for enhancing voter turnout but to projects related to agriculture & food security programme; WASH; Renewable Energy; disaster management and health.\" Jaishankar (BS 2025-02-23) said USAID \"was allowed in India 'in good faith, to do good faith activities'\" and that the government was \"looking into it\". The `d` field's assertion that \"nobody in the controversy called [the bilateral portfolio] interference\" has no source. The government's own publication of the portfolio was its answer to the controversy, and Jaishankar's \"bad faith\" remark was about USAID's activities in general. That sentence is an unsourced negative and should be removed or sourced.",
        "innocentReading": "The ₹825 cr is an obligation (a commitment, not a disbursement) under agreements where DEA is the nodal counterparty. The money is spent through USAID-contracted implementers on agriculture, WASH, renewable energy, disaster and health work. Its size says nothing about influence by either side. It is also not \"audited G2G money\" in GoI's accounts: USAID audits it under US rules, not the CAG. The \"zero election money\" side is an absence of evidence, reported by one investigation, not a documented negative.",
        "reason": "The figure is confirmed exactly: Business Standard/PTI 2025-02-23, quoting the FinMin 2023-24 annual report, says \"an obligation of a total of USD 97 million (about Rs 825 crore) has been made by the US Agency for International Development (USAID) under the seven projects\". The claim still misstates what the figure is. The source describes an obligation for projects \"implemented by USAID in partnership with Government of India\", with DEA as nodal department, not \"USAID money to GoI\". The edge target min:ministry-of-finance therefore asserts a money flow to the ministry that the source does not support. Part of the obligated money plausibly reaches Indian associations as implementing partners, which undermines the claim's contrast between GoI and associations. The claim's other load-bearing parts also fall short. \"Dwarfs zero\" is non-discriminating. \"Nobody called it interference\" is unsourced. \"G2G is audited\" (from innocentReading) overstates the record. The underlying analytic point survives: the government documents only non-election USAID programming in FY24, and no document shows USAID election money in India. So the claim can stay at analytic tier once the flow framing is corrected.",
