        "refuted": true,
        "recommendedTier": "analytic",
        "sourceCheck": "partially",
        "denialFound": "This is an analytic claim, so no party is accused and no denial is needed. The government's position lines up with the claim's first half. MoS Home Nityanand Rai, Lok Sabha, 2020-09-21: the Bill is \"not against NGOs or an attack against a religion or community\" (ThePrint, https://theprint.in/india/fcra-bill-not-against-ngos-meant-to-stop-misuse-of-foreign-funds-says-minister-nityanand-rai/507656/). Contrary position: critics call the law a \"tool to silence\" civil society (Al Jazeera 2020-11-13, https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2020/11/13/restrictions-on-funding-of-indian-civil-society). CPR (c065) objects to its publications being treated as current-affairs programming.",
        "innocentReading": "The file's own reading holds for the statutory text. Parliament adopted a bright-line anti-layering rule in s.7, and Noel Harper upheld it as a remedy for \"endless chain of transfers ... creating layered trail of money\". Under that rule, intermediaries losing out is the design. That reading supports a claim about the text of s.7. It does not support a claim about enforcement outcomes, or about the absence of any orientation effect.",
        "reason": "The claim joins a supported statement to an unsupported negative one. First half (supported): s.7 made the sub-granting intermediary model illegal. PRS: \"The Bill amends this to prohibit the transfer of foreign contribution to any other person.\" Noel Harper: \"a certificate of registration is not granted for acting as an intermediary ... The amended provision, therefore, completely rules out such transfer\". Second half (unsupported): \"not any political orientation\". Neither cited source addresses orientation. The file's own symmetryCheck says the government-aligned control named in the SPEC (VIF, India Foundation, Ekal/Vidya Bharati) \"were not examined in this file\", so the control needed for the negative claim was never run. The supporting sentence in d (\"modal ground is onward transfer / non-designated account\") is contradicted by the file's own records. World Vision's grounds are unspecified (c071). Environics' ground is protest payments (c091). CARE's is pre-2020 account conduct (c067). CPR's includes content grounds (c064). EFI's is \"harmony\" (c076). And c012 names advocacy and research groups as the losers of the s.8 cap. As worded, the claim fails. A narrower version limited to the text of s.7 would stand as analytic.",
        "corrections": [
          "1. Rewrite lab as: \"The 2020 Amendment's s.7 made the sub-granting intermediary model per se unlawful; its textual target is a funding structure, not a viewpoint.\" 2. Drop \"not any political orientation\" until the SPEC control (enforcement against VIF, India Foundation, Ekal/Vidya Bharati affiliates) is run. 3. Replace d with the tally the file actually supports. Of about 12 post-2020 actions with any stated ground, about 3 are transfer/account grounds (Oxfam, CPR in part, CARE, the last for 2012-13 conduct under the 2010 Act) and about 4 carry content or activity grounds (CPR s.3/\"undesirable purposes\", EFI \"harmony\", CASA cluster conversion, Environics protest payments). Remove World Vision (grounds unspecified, c071) and Environics (protest-payment ground, c091) from the transfer list. 4. Add s.8 (20% admin cap, c012) as a second operative loser class: advocacy, research and legal aid. 5. Keep srcs at PRS and Noel Harper for the rewritten statutory claim, and drop the case sources from this edge. 6. Reconcile the target id: the file has ngo:fcra-associations-aggregate, the payload has ngo:fcra-registered-associations. 7. Keep upgradeIf and killIf. Add a note that the 2026 FCRA Amendment Bill (introduced in the Lok Sabha 2026-03-25) should be checked before the edge is left open-ended."
        ],
        "applied": [
          "recorded"
        ]
