        "denialFound": "Compassion, the party affected (Compassion UK blog, https://www.compassionuk.org/blogs/compassion-closes-india-operations/): \"Compassion has broken no laws in India\"; it denies the Indian media's conversion allegation. That response is already recorded as c031 against c030. No MHA statement disputing that funds were blocked was found. The government's stated ground (sub-granting to NGOs without FCRA registration) appears only in search summaries, per c030. No MHA response was found because I did not ask and web search was exhausted. CT 2017 reports that one Indian official told India Today the case could be reconsidered.",
        "innocentReading": "This is a routine FCRA compliance action. The MHA put a large foreign donor that sent about $45m a year straight to local churches through two offices on its prior-approval list, then declined approval, possibly over onward transfers to unregistered partners. The edge stays neutral on motive, which is correct.",
        "reason": "No part of the claim is refuted, but its date support comes from a source the claim does not cite. Every element checks out against a source: funds blocked (Compassion UK), exit on March 15 (Compassion UK and CT 2017), exit year 2017 and US$45m a year (CT 2024: \"brought about $45 million annually into India\"; CT 2017: \"channels about $45 million into the country every year\"). The ₹300 crore conversion is correct arithmetic at the stated, flagged rate of about ₹67/US$ (45 × 67 ≈ 301.5). The 2016 start date and the prior-permission mechanism are not in the two cited sources; they come only from CT 2017-03-01. \"Reported\" is the right tier: the evidence is one credible outlet (CT, 2017 and 2024) plus the affected party's own statement, with no MHA order or Parliament answer opened.",
