        "denialFound": "Yes, and CARE was asked. TOI (2023-06-13) reports that CARE confirmed the notice \"in an email response to TOI\": \"We are working on the clarifications for the government and are confident the temporary suspension will be revoked.\" CARE also said it \"has always complied with regulations\". CARE did not answer TOI's question about scaling down operations. The contra in c068 quotes CSR Universe paraphrases in quotation marks (\"committed to serve the nation\", \"the suspension is temporary and will be revoked soon\"). Those are not CARE's words. Replace them with the TOI quote. URL: https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/mha-suspends-fcra-licence-of-care-india-over-violations/articleshow/100947304.cms",
        "innocentReading": "This looks like a routine regulatory action. The auditor's certificate recorded a Section 17(1) breach: ₹28.3 crore of foreign money received in 2012-13 in a domestic-funds account, with remittance certificates marked \"business and management consultancy and public relations\". The 2022-09 IT survey probably brought this back into view. A 180-day suspension pending inquiry is the standard first step under Section 13. On the other side, CARE had filed a condonation plea with MHA and provided for a ₹5.6 crore penalty in its accounts in 2018. That suggests a compoundable, decade-old lapse was used as the basis for a 2023 suspension, after a renewal granted despite the same lapse.",
        "reason": "The core fact holds up. MHA suspended the FCRA registration of CARE India's registrant, CISSD, for 180 days in June 2023. TOI reported it from government sources, and CARE confirmed receiving the notice in an email to TOI. ET and The CSR Universe both repeat TOI, so there is one original report, not two independent ones. That fits \"reported\", not \"documented\". The claim does not survive as written, though. (1) The date 2023-06-14 is a secondary outlet's dateline; the notice was served no later than 2023-06-12. (2) The only cited source republishes TOI, and its headline wrongly says \"cancels\". (3) The parenthetical \"₹377.5 crore foreign funding 2021-22\" puts an unrelated figure next to the action. The sources link that figure only to the IT survey, not to any ground for suspension. (4) The record's d-field calls the ₹28.3 crore matter the \"Stated grounds\". The sources attribute it to the auditor's certificate, not to an MHA order. The d-field also leaves out the condonation plea and the ₹5.6 crore penalty provision. These are field fixes, not a refutation of the event.",
