        "denialFound": "Partial response on record. CARE India (TOI, carried by indiacsr.in and myind.net): \"We are working on the clarifications for the government and are confident the temporary suspension will be revoked.\" The CSR Universe says the NGO \"believes that the suspension is temporary and will be revoked soon.\" This is already recorded as contra fcra-actions:c068. CARE India did not deny the 2012-13 receipt. It disclosed that receipt itself, through its auditor's certificate, and filed for condonation. No MHA statement of grounds was found. Final status (revoked or cancelled) is not verified.",
        "innocentReading": "The ₹28.3 cr in 2012-13 is a disclosure in CARE India's own auditor's certificate, not a government allegation. The auditor flagged it as a s.17(1) breach, CARE India filed a condonation plea, and it provided a ₹5.6 cr penalty in its 2018 accounts. The news outlets reprinted the auditor's certificate next to the 2023 suspension story, and that placement is the only link between the two. MHA's notice may rest on something else entirely (for example, material from the 2022 IT survey, or FY2021-22 transfers). No source says what the notice's stated grounds were.",
        "reason": "The source does not call this the ground for the suspension. The exact sentence in The CSR Universe (2023-06-15) is: \"Earlier, in 2012-13, CARE India had received a foreign contribution to the tune of Rs 28.3 crore (without a permission from MHA) in a bank account that was meant for local funds. Furthermore, the foreign inward remittance certificate reflects that the NGO received these funds for business, management consultancy and public relations.\" The fuller TOI-derived text (indiacsr.in, myind.net) attributes this to \"the auditor\", not MHA, and adds that \"a condonation plea was later filed with MHA and Rs 5.6 crore penalty provided for in the company's accounts in 2018.\" The claim is therefore a conjunction: a true self-disclosed 2012-13 breach plus a true 2023 suspension, joined into \"MHA's ground\", which no source states. The `alleged` tier is also wrong. No named party is making an allegation here; the fact comes from the subject's own auditor. The edge dates (2012 to 2013) attach MHA enforcement to a period when MHA took no action. As an MHA-to-CARE `enforce` edge, it duplicates c066 and adds an unsupported causal link. Kill the edge, and move the fact, with the condonation plea and the penalty, into c066 as disclosed background.",
