        "denialFound": "This is wrong in the file. The claim text says \"no CASA response recorded\", and contra c075 says \"No response recorded — asked/not asked unknown\". Christianity Today (2024-04-02) does record a CASA response. Bishop Subodh C. Mondal, CASA's vice chairperson: \"CASA's entire work is jeopardized. They had to terminate close to 350 staff in India ... Ninety percent of their work is affected by this. We will apply afresh for FCRA.\" (https://www.christianitytoday.com/2024/04/india-fcra-ngo-world-vision-foreign-funding-close/). It does not rebut any specific ground, and no specific ground was published to rebut. CT also quotes an unnamed Christian NGO leader: reasons for cancellation are \"often unclear and sometimes even illegitimate\". There is also a third-party op-ed rebuttal in Morung Express (2024-04-14), by Rev Rümatho Nyusou. The Wire (2024-04-04) does not say whether CASA was contacted.",
        "innocentReading": "The ministry cancelled five registrations in one order sweep on compliance grounds: use of foreign contributions outside declared objectives, which the FCRA 2020 amendment tightened. The press, working from anonymous \"sources\" (ET, PTI/The Hindu), attached a composite list of grounds to the whole batch. The conversion element may apply to some members, or to none, but not necessarily to CASA. CASA's own reaction was to reapply, which fits a registration or compliance dispute as well as it fits targeting.",
        "reason": "The only cited source (The CSR Universe, 2024-05-01) does not say what the claim says. It attaches \"misuse of foreign funds, and unauthorized activities related to religious conversions\" to a later batch of six NGOs that does not include CASA. For the April five that include CASA, it gives only FCRA/FCRR violations and use of funds \"for purposes other than their declared objectives\". The substance can be rescued from other outlets. Business Standard (2024-04-03) and Swarajya both cite an Economic Times report that the five were revoked over \"alleged involvement in religious conversion, violations of foreign grants\". But that ground is anonymously sourced, applies to the whole batch (including the secular VHAI), and is not specific to CASA. Separately, \"no CASA response recorded\" is false: CT quotes CASA's vice chairperson. The claim fails as written. It can survive only as `alleged`, re-sourced to ET/BS/The Hindu, reworded as a collective ground, and paired with a contra that records the Mondal statement.",
