        "denialFound": "MHA: none found, and it is not known whether MHA was asked. The only statement of MHA's grounds is secondhand in FIDH, describing its 2014 complaint: unaccounted withdrawals of Rs 2.8 million in July 2012 and Rs 16.9 million over 2008–12, and Rs 4.5 million withdrawn on 240 occasions in 2011–13. CPSC: contested the suspensions in the Delhi HC and, per FIDH, won in March 2014. The 2014 judgment was not found on Indian Kanoon, which lists only CPSC orders from 2024–25.",
        "innocentReading": "MHA inspected CPSC's accounts in 2012, found what it treated as cash withdrawals and fund transfers it could not account for, and suspended the registration under s.13 pending inquiry. It re-suspended when the inquiry ran past 180 days, and the court then ended the practice. That is routine regulatory process, not retaliation. The claim as worded implies no motive, so this reading fits it.",
        "reason": "The substance is confirmed word for word by the only cited source. FIDH 2022-01-14: \"between 2012 and 2014, CPSC had its FCRA registration suspended on three occasions, for a total period of 18 months, until the Delhi High Court ruled in CPSC's favor in March 2014 following a writ petition by CPSC, which had challenged the suspension of its FCRA registration.\" The date test passes because everything falls within UPA's term. The claim is not refuted, but it adds a precise start date (2012-07-16) and a minister handover (Chidambaram → Shinde) that the source does not contain. Tier stays 'reported', not 'documented': FIDH is an advocacy body aligned with CPSC, the sentence is unsourced (\"The Observatory recalls\"), the source has date errors elsewhere, and the 2014 HC judgment has not been retrieved. A structural problem: c082 is marked supersededBy c083, but c083 is a different edge (Delhi HC → MHA, covering the ruling only). If the supersession stands, the graph loses the MHA → CPSC suspension edge, which is the UPA-era side of the control SPEC requires.",
