        "denialFound": "CPR's response is already in the claim and is verbatim in the Tribune: \"The basis of this decision is incomprehensible and disproportionate and some of the reasons given challenge the very basis of the functioning of a research institution. This includes the publication on our website of policy reports emanating from our research being equated with current affairs programming.\" CPR said it would seek legal remedy. It then challenged the cancellation in the Delhi HC, and Justice Chandra Dhari Singh issued notice to the Centre (Bar & Bench, 26 Mar 2024: https://www.barandbench.com/news/delhi-high-court-seeks-governments-reply-plea-centre-for-policy-research-fcra-cancellation). MHA's side, via officials quoted by PTI (https://www.theweek.in/wire-updates/national/2024/01/17/del65--mha-fcra-2ndld-cpr.html): CPR was \"misutilising foreign funds to affect India's economic interests, and aid protests and legal battles against development projects\"; it \"diverted foreign donation to non-FCRA entity\"; and \"Publishing current affairs programme using foreign funding is prohibited under section 3 of the Act\". The claim does not include these grounds.",
        "innocentReading": "This is a routine regulatory action under FCRA sections 3, 8 and 14, after a year of suspension and an Income Tax survey. MHA gave specific grounds: transfers to non-FCRA entities, funding litigation and paralegals against coal projects, and publishing 'current affairs' content. CPR disputes those grounds, and the dispute is before the Delhi HC. The edge only records that the enforcement happened. It does not say whether the grounds were valid or the action was proportionate, so it should not be read as proving harassment.",
