        "denialFound": "The ministers were asked and did not reply. The Wire: \"The Wire asked Nirmala Sitharaman, 'Would you accept that there is a conflict of interest…' Similar questions were put to Prabhu, Akbar and Sinha. Till the time of publication, none of them chose to reply.\" The PMO (Nripendra Misra) was also asked and did not answer. India Foundation denied the allegations in a statement on its homepage, as reported by The Quint on 2017-11-05 (https://www.thequint.com/news/hot-news/ajit-dovals-son-shaurya-conflict-of-interest-the-wire). It called the article \"speculative and clearly intends to insinuate wrong-doing where there is none.\" It said the directors named \"were members of the Foundation long before they were made Ministers,\" that \"no foreign funding had been received,\" and that the directors had not \"furthered the commercial or private interests of any company.\" Shaurya Doval told The Wire: \"There is no conflict of interest as India Foundation does not conduct any commercial transactions… Its charter does not include lobbying.\" The Wire replied the same day (Siddharth Varadarajan, https://thewire.in/politics/shaurya-doval-response-the-wire-questions-answers-india-foundation). The record currently contains none of these responses.",
        "innocentReading": "Sitharaman and the others joined the board of a policy think tank close to their party before they entered government, as the foundation says. Staying on an unpaid advisory-style board is common for politicians with policy interests. Sponsorship of conferences, possibly received through a partner organisation such as FICCI, is legally different from a foreign contribution to the trust under FCRA. The Wire offers no evidence that any minister made a decision that favoured a sponsor. The piece is a propriety or appearance-of-conflict argument, supported by two anonymous former officials (a defence secretary and a cabinet secretary), not a finding of wrongdoing.",
        "reason": "The source is reachable. The full article body is in the page's JSON-LD articleBody, so the record's note that it \"could not render The Wire article body\" is out of date. The article confirms the core role edge: on 2017-11-04, India Foundation listed Defence Minister Nirmala Sitharaman among its directors, and she was asked about it and did not reply. That supports tier \"reported\": one credible outlet, with a published screenshot of the directors page. The claim text as written is wrong, though. It attributes to The Wire two things the article does not say: that the four directors \"became Union ministers in 2014\" (false for M.J. Akbar, who became a minister in 2016) and that they \"stayed on.\" It also cites a Wikipedia paraphrase as the effective source. The \"stayed on\" part is in fact supported, but by India Foundation's own denial statement, which the record leaves out. The claim is refuted as worded. It should survive only in corrected form, still as \"reported.\"",
