        "denialFound": "None found for INC. Not asked. The government's stated reason for the change was to align the FCRA foreign-source definition with FEMA foreign-investment limits. That reason is visible in the proviso wording the SC judgment reproduces (\"where the nominal value of share capital is within the limits specified for foreign investment under the Foreign Exchange Management Act, 1999 ... such company shall not be a foreign source\"). No ministerial statement was opened this session. Web search budget was exhausted, and thehindu.com and deccanherald.com both blocked fetching (403 or refused).",
        "innocentReading": "The 2016 amendment is a general, forward-looking alignment of FCRA with FEMA, backdated only to the date FCRA 2010 was enacted. Its aim was that Indian companies with FDI inside permitted limits would not be treated as foreign sources for any recipient, not only for political parties. Whatever its motive, its 2010 effective date meant it did not cover the pre-2010 Vedanta-subsidiary donations. It only became relevant to the Delhi HC finding once the 2018 Finance Act moved the date back to 1976.",
        "reason": "The only cited source is the Wikipedia \"Electoral bonds\" article (Electoral_Bond_Scheme now redirects there). It does not say what the claim says. The exact sentence is: \"A comparable instance of the misuse of the Money Bill was seen previous to the retrospective amendments made to the Foreign Currency Regulation Act (FCRA) by the government, on two separate occasions, in an effort to shield the BJP and the Congress from prosecution for FCRA violations, as determined by the Delhi High Court.\"\n\nThree problems follow.\n- It never names 2016 and never says either amendment actually shielded anyone. It says \"in an effort to,\" which is a claim about intent.\n- The citation behind that sentence (ref \"JSCdh\") is a 5 Feb 2019 Deccan Herald opinion column by Jagdeep S. Chhokar, a co-founder of ADR, the petitioner in the Delhi HC case. That makes it a named, interested party's claim, not a credible outlet with named sourcing. A Wikipedia article is also not an eligible \"reported\" source under CONTRACT.md.\n- On the file's own facts (2016 effective date 2010-09-26; donations up to 2009 under FCRA 1976), the 2016 amendment could not have shielded INC from the Delhi HC finding. The claim combines two true facts, \"two amendments\" and \"shield effort,\" into a 2016-specific effect that no source states.\n\nOn top of that, c024 is already marked supersededBy political-trusts:c026, the 2018 edge, which is the correct place for this symmetric claim.",
