        "denialFound": "Yes, three responses. (1) India Foundation statement by Shaurya Doval, 4 Nov 2017, reproduced in full at https://thewire.in/politics/shaurya-doval-response-the-wire-questions-answers-india-foundation and covered at https://www.thequint.com/news/hot-news/ajit-dovals-son-shaurya-conflict-of-interest-the-wire. It calls the article \"speculative and clearly intends to insinuate wrong-doing where there is none\". It says the directors were associated with IF before becoming ministers and that \"No foreign funding has ever been received by the Foundation from any overseas private corporation or individual\". It says \"The Foundation and its Directors have not furthered the commercial or private interests of any company\". It says the Foundation \"is in complete compliance with all statutory requirements\". The four ministers were sent questionnaires and did not reply. (2) The Caravan: Vivek Doval, Shaurya Doval and Ajit Doval were all sent questionnaires and did not respond. Vivek Doval then filed criminal defamation against The Caravan, Kaushal Shroff and Jairam Ramesh, alleging \"inferences and innuendos\" implying money laundering and round-tripping. (3) Indian Express, 20 Dec 2020 (https://indianexpress.com/article/india/defamation-case-jairam-ramesh-apologises-to-vivek-doval-7111783/): Ramesh apologised \"for any hurt the statements may have caused\", and Doval's lawyer said \"proceedings against The Caravan and Shroff will continue\". Final outcome not found; web search budget exhausted.",
        "innocentReading": "India Foundation is a registered trust whose founding directors happened to become ministers and stayed on its board, which is an impropriety of appearance rather than proof of favours. It pays for events through conference sponsorship. Its leadership says that is not a \"foreign contribution\", and Indian trusts generally do not publish donor lists. Separately, Vivek Doval is a UK-resident fund manager who set up a small ($11.19m per a July 2018 SEC filing) Asia equities fund in the Cayman Islands, the standard domicile for such funds. It shares staff with his brother's Singapore/India asset-management firms, which is ordinary for family businesses in one sector. None of this involves India Foundation's money.",
        "reason": "The claim as worded is a conjunction that implies something none of its sources say. The Wire (4 Nov 2017, opened and quoted) supports \"opaque financials\" (\"it refuses to part with any information about its source of revenue\") and alleged conflicts of interest from ministers serving as directors. The Caravan (16 Jan 2019, opened; body rendered) supports only a Cayman fund run by Vivek Doval that is \"linked inextricably to businesses run by his brother Shaurya\". Nothing in it connects that fund to India Foundation. \"Undisclosed\" is also unsupported: the fund was traced through SEC, Companies House and Cayman filings. The \"tied to it\" link to India Foundation is the load-bearing inference, and it is source-does-not-say. \"Doval family runs\" makes a family the actor, which the contract bars from edges and allows only as a calibrated narrative. The first two limbs survive at reported (one credible outlet with published documents, with IF's denial on record). The offshore limb survives only as \"Vivek Doval runs a Cayman fund with administrative overlaps with Shaurya Doval's firms\", tier reported and contested by a defamation suit still pending against The Caravan as of Dec 2020.",
