        "sourceCheck": "partially",
        "denialFound": "Government response recorded (ThePrint/PTI, 18 Sep 2026, https://theprint.in/india/fcra-registered-ngos-down-by-half-in-last-10-years-contribution-rises-from-rs-17832-cr-to-rs-22974-cr/3046874/): \"The government has rejected the charges and challenged the opposition parties to identify even one provision that discriminates against minorities. The government has also maintained that the proposed legislation is not religion-specific.\" In the same report, Rai said the Bill is \"indeed dangerous\" for those engaged in \"forced religious conversion\" using foreign contribution. PIB factsheet, 22 Jul 2026: the Authority \"cannot convert\" places of worship, and \"No official benefits personally.\" The narrative does not carry this response and should.",
        "innocentReading": "About 35,000 registrations were cancelled or ceased over a decade, leaving FC assets \"in limbo, which States said they could not take possession of, maintain or manage under the bare Section 15\" (PIB). On this reading the Bill supplies a custody and disposal procedure for a vesting power that already existed. It returns assets if registration is restored, protects the religious character of places of worship, and cuts the maximum prison term from 5 years to 1. The plain explanation is administrative clean-up of a legal gray zone, not confiscation aimed at churches.",
        "reason": "The narrative survives as a contested item because every component is in primary or near-primary sources. PRS says: \"The Bill creates a Designated Authority for the vesting, supervision, management and disposal of foreign contribution and assets ... the Authority must ensure that its religious character is maintained. The Bill reduces the maximum penalty ... from imprisonment of five years to one year.\" PRS also confirms proceeds are \"credited to the Consolidated Fund of India\". PIB confirms s.15 has vested assets \"since 2010\". Two problems prevent a clean pass. (1) The strongestCounter uses PIB's framing, which PRS contradicts. PIB says cessation already triggers vesting, but PRS says \"The Bill adds that cessation of the certificate will also lead to vesting of assets.\" PRS also identifies other new elements: permanent vesting and sale; an asset \"created or acquired partly by foreign funds ... shall entirely vest\"; no way to exit FCRA without losing assets; the Rs 10 lakh two-year utilisation rule under the 2026 Rules; and no appeal or hearing when renewal is denied. \"s.15 has existed since 2010\" is therefore true but understates what changes. (2) The strongestCase joins two figures that do not belong together. It cites Rs 35,968 cr of unutilised balances as the \"pool\" at risk, but PTI gives that figure as total unutilised FC for 2024-25 (FDs, utilisation accounts and designated accounts), which mostly sits with active filers. No source says it belongs to cancelled or ceased registrants.",
